13	/* Note on local operation return types:
14	
15	    The local operations all return a std::optional<T> type. This is used so that your
16	    implementation can signify that it cannot perform an operation (i.e., because
17	    the resulting mesh does not have a valid representation).
18	
19	    An optional can have two values: std::nullopt, or a value of the type it is
20	    parameterized on. In this way, it's similar to a pointer, but has two advantages:
21	    the value it holds need not be allocated elsewhere, and it provides an API that
22	    forces the user to check if it is null before using the value.
23	
24	    In your implementation, if you have successfully performed the operation, you can
25	    simply return the required reference:
26	
27	            ... collapse the edge ...
28	            return collapsed_vertex_ref;
29	
30	    And if you wish to deny the operation, you can return the null optional:
31	
32	            return std::nullopt;
33	
34	    Note that the stubs below all reject their duties by returning the null optional.
35	*/
